{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf8QDytiyEqDXCFYzzonG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HN1jNYEJDz4nvBSkve3xHw","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/workspace/trials/RESEARCH-LOG.md","old_string":"A **194×** reduction — and it frees 18.2% of the fixed 64,000-exposure budget\nfor rows that can actually be separated.","new_string":"A **194×** reduction — and it frees 18.2% of the fixed 64,000-exposure budget\nfor rows that can actually be separated.\n\nMeasured exactly on the two **actually compiled** 64,000-exposure schedules\n(not expectations — the real `schedule.json` contents):\n\n| Per update | T1 uniform | T2 filtered |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| pos–pos duplicate pairs (mean) | 0.849 | 0.009 |\n| updates with ≥1 such pair | 50.4% | 0.9% |\n| pos-as-negative pairs (mean) | 1.139 | 0.005 |\n| **queries with a corrupted target** | **2.11 / 64 (3.30%)** | **0.02 / 64 (0.04%)** |\n| worst single update | 10 / 64 | 2 / 64 |\n| implied irreducible mean-loss floor | **0.0229** | **0.0002** |\n\nSo in T1 roughly one update in two contains at least one query whose correct\nanswer is duplicated among the passages it is being trained to rank against."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2270,"cache_read_input_tokens":124147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2270},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"0d68b458-f2f6-49dc-821b-9996b6f2899a","request_id":"req_011Cf8QDyTfgmNmV1hcccSqS"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HN1jNYEJDz4nvBSkve3xHw","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/trials/RESEARCH-LOG.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"beaa28c9-8220-4cf9-a428-9673e0fe849b","timestamp":"2026-09-17T04:44:23.722Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/trials/RESEARCH-LOG.md","oldString":"A **194×** reduction — and it frees 18.2% of the fixed 64,000-exposure budget\nfor rows that can actually be separated.","newString":"A **194×** reduction — and it frees 18.2% of the fixed 64,000-exposure budget\nfor rows that can actually be separated.\n\nMeasured exactly on the two **actually compiled** 64,000-exposure schedules\n(not expectations — the real `schedule.json` contents):\n\n| Per update | T1 uniform | T2 filtered |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| pos–pos duplicate pairs (mean) | 0.849 | 0.009 |\n| updates with ≥1 such pair | 50.4% | 0.9% |\n| pos-as-negative pairs (mean) | 1.139 | 0.005 |\n| **queries with a corrupted target** | **2.11 / 64 (3.30%)** | **0.02 / 64 (0.04%)** |\n| worst single update | 10 / 64 | 2 / 64 |\n| implied irreducible mean-loss floor | **0.0229** | **0.0002** |\n\nSo in T1 roughly one update in two contains at least one query whose correct\nanswer is duplicated among the passages it is being trained to rank against.","originalFile":"# ReasonIR difficulty-conditioned curriculum — research log\n\nAll measurements below are derived **only** from the fixed pool\n(`/opt/reasonir-task/train/pool.parquet`) and the fixed trainer's own constants.\nNo evaluator, benchmark, corpus, or external data was consulted.\n\n## Fixed-pool structure (offline, CPU only)\n\n| Property | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Rows | 345,491 (hq 100,521 / vl 244,970) |\n| Positives / negatives per row | exactly 1 / exactly 1 |\n| Queries | all 345,491 unique |\n| Distinct positive texts | 270,552 (max multiplicity **2,963**) |\n| Distinct negative texts | 278,013 (max multiplicity 3,439) |\n| Texts that are a positive somewhere *and* a negative elsewhere | 4,646 |\n\n### The degenerate block\n\nA quarter of the pool is **classification data mis-shaped as retrieval**. For\n87,235 rows (25.3%) the \"positive document\" is a bare class label and the\n\"negative document\" is another bare class label (median negative length **9\ncharacters**), with an empty query instruction:\n\n```\nQ: 'Dear valued customer, we are pleased to inform you that you have been pre-approved…'\nP: 'spam'          N: 'legitimate email from a financial institution'\nQ: 'The sociolinguistic context in which we communicate affects how we express ourselves…'\nP: 'English'       N: 'Linguistics discussion in academic setting'\n```\n\nTop shared positives: `positive` (2,963 rows), `English` (2,446), `neutral`\n(2,440), `formal` (2,149), `spam` (1,658), `sarcastic` (1,425), `hate speech` (885).\n\nThe separation from real retrieval rows is clean, not a judgement call:\n\n| positive length | mult 1 | mult 2–9 | mult 10–99 | mult ≥100 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ≤40 chars | 12,702 | 11,729 | **21,413** | **41,391** |\n| 41–100 | 23,602 | 60 | 10 | 0 |\n| 101–200 | 45,380 | 68 | 0 | 0 |\n| 201–500 | 52,541 | 1,262 | 0 | 0 |\n| >500 | 130,085 | 5,238 | 10 | 0 |\n\nEvery row whose positive is shared by ≥10 queries has a positive ≤40 chars.\nThe complement's multiplicity p99 is 4. Short-but-*unique* positives (mult 1)\nare legitimate document titles (\"Refrigerator Troubleshooting Guide\", \"Fungal\nCell Wall\") and are **kept**.\n\n## Mechanism hypothesis (H1)\n\nThe fixed trainer computes cross-entropy over a 64-query × 128-passage matrix at\n**τ = 0.02**, so a 0.02 cosine gap is one logit. Two consequences:\n\n1. **Gradient mass concentrates on high-loss rows.** T1's loss is already 0.09\n   by update 50, so the great majority of exposures contribute ≈0 gradient and\n   the update direction is set by the minority of unseparated rows.\n2. **Label-anchor rows are permanently unseparable.** When two of the 64 queries\n   in an update draw the same label string, that string appears twice among the\n   128 passages with an identical embedding, so the target probability is capped\n   near 0.5 regardless of the parameters. Their loss is irreducible, so they\n   retain large gradient forever and claim a disproportionate share of the\n   update direction — and that direction pulls unrelated queries onto a handful\n   of shared label anchors, which is metric-space collapse.\n\nExpected contradictory passage pairs per update (computed exactly from text\nmultiplicities, batch size 64):\n\n| Pool | pos–pos | pos-as-negative | total |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Full (T1, uniform) | 0.800 | 1.066 | **1.866** |\n| Label-anchor filtered (T2) | 0.0086 | 0.0010 | **0.0096** |\n\nA **194×** reduction — and it frees 18.2% of the fixed 64,000-exposure budget\nfor rows that can actually be separated.\n\n> **H1.** Excluding label-anchor rows from the fixed pool raises the fraction of\n> the fixed exposure budget that yields coherent, reducible gradient, and removes\n> the dominant source of geometry-collapsing gradient. At equal budget this\n> should improve general retrieval `G` at least as much as reasoning `R`.\n\n### Falsifiable predictions\n\n- **P1** (internal, free): T2's training loss is lower and smoother than T1's at\n  matched update counts, because the irreducible noise floor is removed.\n- **P2**: `G(T2) ≥ G(T1)`, and the `G` gap is at least as large as the `R` gap.\n  *Falsified if `G(T2) ≤ G(T1)`* — that would refute the collapse mechanism.\n- **P3**: `R(T2) ≥ R(T1)`.\n\n### Known confound and its control\n\nThe filter removes only `vl` rows, so hq exposure share moves 29.1% → 35.4%.\nA skeptic could attribute any gain to \"more hq\". **T3** is the ratio-matched\ncontrol: sample the *unfiltered* pool with source weights reproducing 35.4% hq\nwhile keeping the label-anchor rows. If `T2 > T3 ≈ T1`, the mechanism is\ndegeneracy removal, not mixture ratio.\n\n## Filter definition (policy surface: `include`)\n\nA row is a **label-anchor row** iff its positive text is ≤40 characters *and* is\nthe gold positive for ≥10 distinct queries in the fixed pool.\n→ 62,804 rows excluded (18.18%); 0 are `hq`. Result is insensitive to the\nthreshold (≥100 would still exclude 41,391).\n\n## Trials\n\n| Trial | Policy | Method | Pool | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| t01-uniform | p01-uniform-control | uniform | full 345,491 | running |\n| t02-label-anchor-filter | p02-label-anchor-filter | uniform | 282,687 | queued |\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":74,"oldLines":6,"newStart":74,"newLines":21,"lines":[" A **194×** reduction — and it frees 18.2% of the fixed 64,000-exposure budget"," for rows that can actually be separated."," ","+Measured exactly on the two **actually compiled** 64,000-exposure schedules","+(not expectations — the real `schedule.json` contents):","+","+| Per update | T1 uniform | T2 filtered |","+| --- | --- | --- |","+| pos–pos duplicate pairs (mean) | 0.849 | 0.009 |","+| updates with ≥1 such pair | 50.4% | 0.9% |","+| pos-as-negative pairs (mean) | 1.139 | 0.005 |","+| **queries with a corrupted target** | **2.11 / 64 (3.30%)** | **0.02 / 64 (0.04%)** |","+| worst single update | 10 / 64 | 2 / 64 |","+| implied irreducible mean-loss floor | **0.0229** | **0.0002** |","+","+So in T1 roughly one update in two contains at least one query whose correct","+answer is duplicated among the passages it is being trained to rank against.","+"," > **H1.** Excluding label-anchor rows from the fixed pool raises the fraction of"," > the fixed exposure budget that yields coherent, reducible gradient, and removes"," > the dominant source of geometry-collapsing gradient. At equal budget this"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
