{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YKKGhewbntStJWL6P7vnza","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/trials/diagnostics/read_ordering_axis.py has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"9e7c89a1-24a3-4fbf-9418-6f8d7168e8b6","timestamp":"2026-09-18T03:52:09.110Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/trials/diagnostics/read_ordering_axis.py","oldString":"    Each band is exactly one curriculum phase, i.e. exactly one difficulty decile.\n    Interpolation matters (limit 6): p13 samples every ~45 updates, so its band\n    edges are estimated rather than observed.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = [u for u, _ in t]\n    y = [v for _, v in t]","newString":"    Each band is exactly one curriculum phase, i.e. exactly one difficulty decile.\n    Interpolation matters (limit 6): p13 samples every ~45 updates, so its band\n    edges are estimated rather than observed.\n\n    ANCHORED AT (0, 0.0). peft initialises lora_B to zeros, so SCALE*(B@A) is\n    EXACTLY zero before the first update -- a fact about the adapter, not an\n    assumption. Without the anchor f(0) clamps to the FIRST SAMPLE's value: 0.395\n    for p13 (u=33), 0.211 for p14 (u=19), 0.446 for p15 (u=38). Band 0's gain then\n    came out understated by 25%, 11% and 26% -- a DIFFERENT amount per arm, because\n    each tracker happened to start at a different update. That is a systematic,\n    arm-dependent bias sitting in the one cell the sep-9 matched pair rests on\n    (item 99).\n    \"\"\"\n    t = [(0, 0.0)] + [p for p in t if p[0] > 0]\n    x = [u for u, _ in t]\n    y = [v for _, v in t]","originalFile":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The ordering axis, read across BOTH arms. POST-HOC -- see the label below.\n\nWritten 02:40, after p14 scored. p13's and p14's readings were pre-registered and\nare in read_p13.py / read_p14.py; those are the ones that carry inferential weight.\nTHIS file is exploratory. It exists because p14's result broke the registered\naccount in a specific, informative way, and the break is worth measuring rather\nthan narrating.\n\nWHAT THE TWO ARMS DID (T2 = the same rows in random order; only time differs):\n\n    arm    order              R          G\n    T2     random        0.21776    0.38126\n    p13    easy-first    0.22340    0.38196     R +0.00564   G +0.00070\n    p14    hard-first    0.20748    0.36262     R -0.01028   G -0.01864\n\nTHE REGISTERED ACCOUNT, AND HOW IT DIED. read_p13.py registered an LR-phase\nmechanism: the LR schedule decays, hq rows are 5.94x easier and train R, vl rows\ntrain G; so easy-first spends high LR on hq (R up) and delivers vl at low LR\n(G down). p14 is that account's mirror. Its scorecard over four directional calls:\n\n    p13  R up    PREDICTED, OBSERVED    hit\n    p13  G down  PREDICTED, OBSERVED +0.00070 (flat/up)    MISS\n    p14  R down  PREDICTED, OBSERVED    hit\n    p14  G up    PREDICTED, OBSERVED -0.01864    MISS, and large\n\n  2/2 on R, 0/2 on G. That is not a model that is half right; a coin is 2/4. What\n  it is, is a model with a real R component and NO G component -- and the G misses\n  share a sign. BOTH orderings put G at or below random order. Phasing does not\n  trade G for R. It spends G.\n\nTHE DAMAGE ACCOUNT, TESTED AND HALF-REFUTED. If hard rows arriving while LR is\nhigh displace the parameters further than the decayed tail can walk back, then\np14 is not \"p13 reversed\", it is DAMAGE -- and damage predicts:\n\n    (D1) p14's displacement should end LARGER than p13's.       REFUTED.\n         5.582 vs 8.420 -- hard-first ends 0.66x as far out, not further.\n    (D2) p13's early phase, on rows whose gradients are ~0 for 76.1% of the kept\n         set, should be nearly FLAT.                            SUPPORTED.\n         12.3% of final displacement by update 100, vs 24.9% for hard-first.\n\nDisplacement is also not incoherent in p14: fitting ||dW|| ~ u^alpha gives 0.683\nfor hard-first against 0.684 for random and 0.811 for easy-first. Hard-first walks\nas straight as random. It simply walks LESS FAR. No arm's norm ever decreases\n(0/22, 0/21, 0/60 samples), so nothing is being un-learned either.\n\nThe competing read is recency/format-match -- whatever the last phase looks like\nis what the model ends up matching. It gets G from the final phase (p13 ends on\nvl, the G-shaped rows; p14 ends on hq) but predicts R(p14) UP, since p14 ends on\nthe hq rows carrying BRIGHT's reasoning instruction. R(p14) is DOWN at 3.58 sigma.\nRecency is refuted on R before the drift data is opened.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY EXPLAINS IT: SATURATION. AND THERE IS NO LR GRADIENT AT ALL.\n\n*** CORRECTED 07:15. Everything this section said about the learning rate was\n*** wrong, because the schedule is not what I assumed. It is\n*** get_constant_schedule_with_warmup(num_warmup_steps=60): the LR ramps 0 -> peak\n*** over 60 updates and is then CONSTANT for the remaining 940. Simulated mean LR\n*** by band: band 0 = 0.695, bands 1-9 = 1.0000 EXACTLY. There is no decay.\n***\n*** So \"high LR is a scarce resource\", \"easy rows are LR-sensitive\", \"the decayed\n*** tail\", and the dose-response \"in the LR gap\" are all retracted (item 98). The\n*** premise entered at read_p13.py (\"the LR schedule is FIXED and decaying\") and was\n*** never checked against train_trial.py, which states it in one line.\n\nThe correction makes the result STRONGER, not weaker, because it removes the\nconfound this file declared as its primary limit rather than deepening it.\n\nTotal displacement rank-orders BOTH metrics across all three arms, with no\ninversions: easy-first 8.025 > random 6.855 > hard-first 5.371, and R and G run\nin the same order. Ordering is not redistributing capacity between R and G. It is\nchanging how much the adapter learns at all.\n\nThe crossed design says why. Each 100-update band is exactly one decile, and p13\nand p14 are mirror images, so every decile is observed in TWO time slots. Matching\non |band separation| is still required -- an unmatched comparison confuses \"how\nearly\" with \"how far apart\" -- but the separation is now a gap in TIME, not in LR:\n\n    sep   easy decile  early/late    hard decile  early/late   ratio   same LR?\n     9        d0          10.85          d9          1.14       9.51   no (warmup)\n     7        d1           6.56          d8          1.00       6.56   YES\n     5        d2           4.60          d7          1.24       3.71   YES\n     3        d3           2.80          d6          1.38       2.03   YES\n     1        d4           1.10          d5          1.66       0.67   YES\n\nFOUR OF THE FIVE PAIRS SIT AT IDENTICAL LEARNING RATE and the gradient survives:\n6.56, 3.71, 2.03, 0.67 as the time gap falls 7, 5, 3, 1. Only the sep-9 pair\ntouches the warmup band, and it is the one that needs a caveat now.\n\nThe cleanest single number is decile 0: it yields 1.183 at band 0 and 0.109 at\nband 9 -- 10.8x MORE displacement while receiving 30% LESS learning rate (0.695 vs\n1.000). LR does not merely fail to explain that; it runs the WRONG WAY. And decile\n9, measured at two bands of identical LR, yields 2.418 at band 1 (p15) against\n1.460 at band 9 (p13), a 1.66x decline.\n\nAt constant LR, a decile contributing less the later it arrives can only be about\nwhat is LEFT TO LEARN. That is SATURATION, and the easy/hard asymmetry is that easy\nmaterial saturates almost completely (decile 0 retains 9% of its early value) while\nhard material barely does (decile 9 retains 60%).\n\nSo the ledger for hard-first is: it spends the opening on rows that stay learnable\nanyway, and spends the tail on rows that by then have nothing left to give (decile\n0 gets 0.109 where it could have had 1.183). Summed over deciles that is the entire\n8.025 - 5.371 = 2.654 gap.\n\nThis also explains the ASYMMETRY that made p14 worth running: the mis-spent\nresource is one-sided. Easy rows late are nearly useless; hard rows late are nearly\nfine. Reversing a curriculum costs more than ordering it correctly gains.\n\nHONEST LIMITS, and they are heavy here:\n\n  1. POST-HOC. No threshold in this file was fixed before the data existed. It\n     cannot confirm anything; it can only fail to support, or quantify. Nothing\n     here is a Judge input and nothing here changes what is staged.\n  2. n=1 per ordering. sigma_R (0.00203) and sigma_G (0.00315) both have 2 df and\n     come from the UNIFORM lottery, not from replicated orderings. They bound the\n     noise of a draw, not of an ordering.\n  3. Drift cadence differs by arm (T2 23 samples, p13 22, p14 62) because the\n     finer tracker was added mid-p14. Trajectory SHAPE is comparable; any\n     statistic that counts samples is not, and none is computed.\n  4. The proxy is ~tier order (rho +0.2807 on `source` vs +0.2444 composite), so\n     \"hard-first\" is in practice \"vl-first\". Difficulty and tier are not separable\n     in this pool and this file does not pretend otherwise.\n  5. DISPLACEMENT IS NOT QUALITY. ||dW|| rank-ordering R and G across three arms\n     is n=3; a perfect rank match on one metric is p = 1/6 by chance, and R and G\n     are not independent. The crossed decile table is the load-bearing evidence,\n     not the three-arm ranking.\n  6. The band gains are INTERPOLATED onto 100-update boundaries from 22 (p13) and\n     61 (p14) samples. p13's ~45-update cadence means its band edges are estimated,\n     not observed. A band-level statistic is therefore a smoothed quantity; the\n     matched-separation pattern is robust to this, a single band is not.\n  7. The first bug I made reading this table compared each decile's two slots\n     WITHOUT matching band separation, which silently re-measures the LR schedule\n     and made all ten deciles look alike. Matched separation is the whole design.\n\nUsage: read_ordering_axis.py\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nT2, T13, T14 = (\"t02-label-anchor-filter\", \"t13-easy-to-hard-phased\",\n                \"t14-hard-to-easy-phased\")\nUNIFORM = (\"t01-uniform\", \"t05-uniform-replicate\", \"t12-uniform-replicate-2\")\nARMS = ((T2, \"random\"), (T13, \"easy-first\"), (T14, \"hard-first\"))\n\n\ndef traj(trial: str) -> list:\n    \"\"\"(update, frobenius_total) merged over both trackers, deduped, sorted.\"\"\"\n    seen: dict[int, float] = {}\n    for name in (\"drift.jsonl\", \"drift-fine.jsonl\"):\n        f = Path(f\"/workspace/trials/{trial}/{name}\")\n        if not f.is_file():\n            continue\n        for line in f.read_text().splitlines():\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            r = json.loads(line)\n            seen.setdefault(r[\"completed_updates\"], r[\"frobenius_total\"])\n    return sorted(seen.items())\n\n\ndef at(t: list, u: int):\n    \"\"\"Displacement at the last sample on or before update u.\"\"\"\n    prior = [v for k, v in t if k <= u]\n    return prior[-1] if prior else None\n\n\ndef bands(t: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Displacement GAIN in each 100-update band, interpolated onto the edges.\n\n    Each band is exactly one curriculum phase, i.e. exactly one difficulty decile.\n    Interpolation matters (limit 6): p13 samples every ~45 updates, so its band\n    edges are estimated rather than observed.\n    \"\"\"\n    x = [u for u, _ in t]\n    y = [v for _, v in t]\n    import bisect\n\n    def f(u):\n        i = bisect.bisect_left(x, u)\n        if i == 0:\n            return y[0]\n        if i >= len(x):\n            return y[-1]\n        if x[i] == u:\n            return y[i]\n        w = (u - x[i - 1]) / (x[i] - x[i - 1])\n        return y[i - 1] + w * (y[i] - y[i - 1])\n\n    # None for bands past the last sample. The clamp makes an unreached band\n    # evaluate to y[-1] - y[-1] = 0.000, which is a LEGAL gain value and so is\n    # indistinguishable from a measurement. Harmless for T2/p13/p14, which are all\n    # complete -- but it is the same latent trap that produced a fabricated\n    # six-band verdict in read_p15_posthoc (item 97), so it is closed here too.\n    last = x[-1]\n    return [None if 100 * (k + 1) > last else f(100 * (k + 1)) - f(100 * k)\n            for k in range(10)]\n\n\ndef ratio(e: list, h: list, b: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Decile b's early-slot gain over its late-slot gain.\n\n    p13 trains decile b in band b; p14 trains it in band 9-b. So p13 holds the\n    EARLIER slot only when b < 9-b. Getting this backwards for b >= 5 inverts half\n    the table and hides the entire effect (limit 7).\n    \"\"\"\n    early, late = (e[b], h[9 - b]) if b < 9 - b else (h[9 - b], e[b])\n    return early / late if late else float(\"inf\")\n\n\ndef first_exposure(trial: str, band: int, n: int = 3):\n    \"\"\"Mean logged loss over the first n logged updates of a band.\n\n    The trainer logs every 10 updates, so the earliest available point is 10\n    updates into the band -- not zero training, but the SAME 10 updates in every\n    arm, which is what makes the comparison matched.\n    \"\"\"\n    d = {}\n    f = Path(f\"/workspace/trials/logs/{trial}.log\")\n    if not f.is_file():\n        return None, []\n    for line in f.read_text().splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if line.startswith(\"{\") and '\"loss\"' in line:\n            r = json.loads(line)\n            d[r[\"update\"]] = r[\"loss\"]\n    # The window must SCALE with n. It was fixed at +40 updates, so every n >= 4\n    # returned the identical four points and the \"robustness sweep\" printed four\n    # identical rows that looked like stability. A sweep whose knob stops moving\n    # is not a sweep.\n    us = sorted(u for u in d if 100 * band < u <= 100 * band + 10 * n)[:n]\n    if not us:\n        return None, []\n    return sum(d[u] for u in us) / len(us), us\n\n\ndef results() -> dict:\n    out = {}\n    for f in sorted(Path(\"/workspace/trials/results\").glob(\"*.json\")):\n        d = json.loads(f.read_text())\n        if d.get(\"_arm\"):\n            out[d[\"_arm\"]] = d\n    return out\n\n\ndef sigma(metric: str, res: dict):\n    v = [res[a][metric] for a in UNIFORM if a in res and metric in res[a]]\n    if len(v) < 3:\n        return None\n    m = sum(v) / len(v)\n    return (sum((x - m) ** 2 for x in v) / (len(v) - 1)) ** 0.5\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    res = results()\n    sr, sg = sigma(\"R\", res), sigma(\"G\", res)\n    se_r, se_g = (sr * 2 ** 0.5 if sr else None), (sg * 2 ** 0.5 if sg else None)\n\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"POST-HOC. Registered readings are in read_p13.py / read_p14.py.\")\n    print(\"Nothing here is a Judge input; nothing here changes what is staged.\")\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(f\"\\nsigma_R {sr:.5f}  sigma_G {sg:.5f}  (both 2 df, from the uniform lottery)\")\n    print(f\"two-arm SE: R {se_r:.5f}   G {se_g:.5f}\\n\")\n\n    print(\"SCORES -- same rows, same 64,000 exposures, same seed; only time differs\")\n    print(f\"  {'arm':<12}{'order':<13}{'R':>9}{'':>4}{'G':>9}\")\n    for a, lab in ARMS:\n        d = res.get(a)\n        if not d:\n            continue\n        tag = \"\" if a == T2 else (f\"  R {d['R']-res[T2]['R']:+.5f} \"\n                                 f\"({(d['R']-res[T2]['R'])/se_r:+.2f}s)\"\n                                 f\"   G {d['G']-res[T2]['G']:+.5f} \"\n                                 f\"({(d['G']-res[T2]['G'])/se_g:+.2f}s)\")\n        print(f\"  {a.split('-')[0]:<12}{lab:<13}{d['R']:>9.5f}{'':>4}{d['G']:>9.5f}{tag}\")\n\n    print(\"\\n  Both orderings sit at or BELOW random order on G. Phasing does not\")\n    print(\"  trade G for R -- it spends G. Only easy-first buys anything back.\")\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"D1/D2: is hard-first DAMAGE, or just p13 reversed?\")\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    ts = {a: traj(a) for a, _ in ARMS}\n    if not all(ts.values()):\n        print(\"  missing drift data.\")\n        return\n    print(f\"  {'arm':<12}{'samples':>8}{'@100':>10}{'@300':>10}{'@500':>10}\"\n          f\"{'@1000':>10}{'  early share':>14}\")\n    early = {}\n    for a, lab in ARMS:\n        t = ts[a]\n        fin = t[-1][1]\n        row = [at(t, u) for u in (100, 300, 500, 1000)]\n        early[a] = (row[0] / fin) if (row[0] and fin) else float(\"nan\")\n        cells = \"\".join(f\"{v:>10.3f}\" if v is not None else f\"{'-':>10}\" for v in row)\n        print(f\"  {a.split('-')[0]:<12}{len(t):>8}{cells}{100*early[a]:>13.1f}%\")\n\n    f13, f14 = ts[T13][-1][1], ts[T14][-1][1]\n    print(f\"\\n  D1  final displacement: hard-first {f14:.3f} vs easy-first {f13:.3f}\"\n          f\"  ({f14/f13:.2f}x)\")\n    print(f\"      {'SUPPORTED -- hard-first ends further from base' if f14 > f13 else 'NOT SUPPORTED -- hard-first does not end further out'}\")\n    print(f\"  D2  fraction of final displacement acquired by update 100:\")\n    print(f\"      easy-first {100*early[T13]:.1f}%   hard-first {100*early[T14]:.1f}%\"\n          f\"   random {100*early[T2]:.1f}%\")\n    print(f\"      {'SUPPORTED -- easy-first barely moves while LR is high' if early[T13] < early[T14] else 'NOT SUPPORTED -- easy-first moves as fast or faster early'}\")\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"CROSSED: every decile is observed in TWO time slots\")\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    e, h = bands(ts[T13]), bands(ts[T14])\n    if any(v is None for v in (e or [None]) + (h or [None])):\n        print(\"  an arm is incomplete -- refusing to report partial bands.\")\n        return\n    print(f\"  total banded displacement: easy-first {sum(e):.3f}   \"\n          f\"hard-first {sum(h):.3f}   gap {sum(e)-sum(h):.3f}\")\n    print(\"\\n  MATCHED on |band separation|. NB the separation is a gap in TIME,\")\n    print(\"  not in LR: the schedule is constant-with-warmup, so mean LR is 0.695 in\")\n    print(\"  band 0 and EXACTLY 1.0 in bands 1-9 (item 98). Four of the five pairs\")\n    print(\"  below therefore sit at identical learning rate.\")\n    print(f\"  {'sep':>5}{'easy':>7}{'E/L':>8}{'hard':>7}{'E/L':>8}{'ratio':>8}   LR\")\n    wins = 0\n    for b in range(5):\n        re_, rh = ratio(e, h, b), ratio(e, h, 9 - b)\n        wins += re_ > rh\n        lo = min(b, 9 - b)\n        print(f\"  {abs(2*b-9):>5}{'d%d' % b:>7}{re_:>8.2f}{'d%d' % (9-b):>7}\"\n              f\"{rh:>8.2f}{re_/rh:>8.2f}   {'same LR' if lo >= 1 else 'warmup band'}\")\n    print(f\"\\n  easy decile loses more by being DELAYED in {wins}/5 matched pairs.\")\n    print(\"  The exception is sep=1 -- the smallest TIME gap, where the effect is\")\n    print(\"  expected to vanish. It is the internal control and it behaves.\")\n    print(f\"\\n  easy rows SATURATE: d0 {e[0]:.3f} at band 0 -> {h[9]:.3f} at band 9,\")\n    print(f\"    and band 0 runs at 0.695 of peak LR while band 9 runs at 1.000 --\")\n    print(f\"    {e[0]/h[9]:.1f}x more displacement on 30% LESS learning rate, so LR\")\n    print(f\"    does not explain it; it runs the wrong way.\")\n    print(f\"  hard rows stay learnable:  d9 {h[0]:.3f} at band 0 -> {e[9]:.3f} at band 9\")\n    print(f\"  so hard-first's opening slot buys d9 only {h[0]-e[9]:+.3f},\")\n    print(f\"  while its tail costs d0 {h[9]-e[0]:+.3f}. That trade, summed\")\n    print(f\"  over deciles, IS the {sum(e)-sum(h):.3f} gap.\")\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"TRANSFER: what does the OTHER material already teach each decile?\")\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"  First-exposure loss -- the decile has just arrived and this band has\")\n    print(\"  barely trained it. Crucially this is LR-FREE at the point\")\n    print(\"  of measurement: it reflects the model state built by updates 0..899,\")\n    print(\"  and the LR *at* the probing band cannot affect what is already known.\")\n    print(\"  That is the one comparison in this design where 'low LR' and 'already\")\n    print(\"  learned' come apart.\\n\")\n    print(f\"  {'window':>8}{'d0 fresh':>10}{'d0 late':>9}{'drop':>8}\"\n          f\"{'d9 fresh':>10}{'d9 late':>9}{'drop':>8}{'ratio':>8}\")\n    drops = []\n    for n in (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10):\n        a, _ = first_exposure(T13, 0, n)\n        b, _ = first_exposure(T14, 9, n)\n        c, _ = first_exposure(T14, 0, n)\n        e, _ = first_exposure(T13, 9, n)\n        if None in (a, b, c, e):\n            continue\n        d0, d9 = 1 - b / a, 1 - e / c\n        drops.append((d0, d9))\n        print(f\"  {n:>8}{a:>10.4f}{b:>9.4f}{100*d0:>7.1f}%\"\n              f\"{c:>10.4f}{e:>9.4f}{100*d9:>7.1f}%{d0/d9:>8.2f}\")\n    if drops:\n        r = [x / y for x, y in drops]\n        print(f\"\\n  *** THE RATIO IS NOT ROBUST: it runs {min(r):.2f} to {max(r):.2f}\"\n              f\" across windows,\")\n        print(f\"      and at n=2 it CHANGES SIGN. An earlier version of this tool\")\n        print(f\"      reported n=3 only -- 2.79x, near the most favourable value --\")\n        print(f\"      as '55.9% vs 20.0%, redundancy is real and large'. RETRACTED.\")\n        print(f\"      decile 0's loss is ~0.01-0.03 in BOTH slots, so a RELATIVE drop\")\n        print(f\"      on it is a ratio of two near-zero numbers and is dominated by\")\n        print(f\"      noise between adjacent logged updates.\\n\")\n    print(\"  WHAT IS ROBUST, in LEVELS rather than ratios:\")\n    lv = []\n    for tag, arm, band in ((\"d0 fresh\", T13, 0), (\"d0 late\", T14, 9),\n                           (\"d9 fresh\", T14, 0), (\"d9 late\", T13, 9)):\n        vals = [first_exposure(arm, band, n)[0] for n in (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10)]\n        vals = [v for v in vals if v is not None]\n        lv.append((tag, min(vals), max(vals)))\n        print(f\"    {tag:<9} {min(vals):.4f} to {max(vals):.4f}  across every window\")\n    print(\"\\n  decile 0 is ALREADY SOLVED by the base model -- ~0.02 before a single\")\n    print(\"  update -- so it has almost nothing to teach in either slot. decile 9 is\")\n    print(\"  STILL unsolved after 900 updates of everything else (~0.80 against a\")\n    print(\"  fresh ~1.0-1.3). That gap is ~50x in levels and holds under every\")\n    print(\"  window, which the relative-drop ratio does not.\")\n    print(\"\\n  So the correct statement is NOT 'other deciles teach decile 0'. It is\")\n    print(\"  'decile 0 was never unsolved, and decile 9 is not solved by anything\")\n    print(\"  else'. Weaker than the retracted claim, and it still explains why the\")\n    print(\"  tail of a curriculum is worth so much more to hard material.\")\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 72)\n    print(\"SCORECARD of the registered LR-phase account\")\n    print(\"=\" * 72)\n    for arm, met, pred in ((T13, \"R\", \"up\"), (T13, \"G\", \"down\"),\n                           (T14, \"R\", \"down\"), (T14, \"G\", \"up\")):\n        d = res[arm][met] - res[T2][met]\n        got = \"up\" if d > 0 else \"down\"\n        print(f\"  {arm.split('-')[0]:<6}{met}  predicted {pred:<5} observed \"\n              f\"{got:<5} {d:+.5f}   {'hit' if got == pred else 'MISS'}\")\n    print(\"  -> 2/2 on R, 0/2 on G, and both G misses share a sign. The account has\")\n    print(\"     a real R component and no G component. A coin scores 2/4.\")\n\n    print(\"\\nLIMITS (printed with every result): post-hoc; n=1 per ordering; sigma\")\n    print(\"from the uniform lottery at 2 df; drift cadence differs by arm so only\")\n    print(\"trajectory SHAPE is compared; 'hard' is ~'vl' at rho 0.28.\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":174,"oldLines":7,"newStart":174,"newLines":17,"lines":["     Each band is exactly one curriculum phase, i.e. exactly one difficulty decile.","     Interpolation matters (limit 6): p13 samples every ~45 updates, so its band","     edges are estimated rather than observed.","+","+    ANCHORED AT (0, 0.0). peft initialises lora_B to zeros, so SCALE*(B@A) is","+    EXACTLY zero before the first update -- a fact about the adapter, not an","+    assumption. Without the anchor f(0) clamps to the FIRST SAMPLE's value: 0.395","+    for p13 (u=33), 0.211 for p14 (u=19), 0.446 for p15 (u=38). Band 0's gain then","+    came out understated by 25%, 11% and 26% -- a DIFFERENT amount per arm, because","+    each tracker happened to start at a different update. That is a systematic,","+    arm-dependent bias sitting in the one cell the sep-9 matched pair rests on","+    (item 99).","     \"\"\"","+    t = [(0, 0.0)] + [p for p in t if p[0] > 0]","     x = [u for u, _ in t]","     y = [v for _, v in t]","     import bisect"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAE51WYCTDGunPBHcxee","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ULcfR3kXVqkTeNLQ7v5cGg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/trials; python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"diagnostics/read_p15_posthoc.py\"); s = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"    if len(t) < 2:\n        return None\n    x = [u for u, _ in t]\n    y = [v for _, v in t]\n    last = x[-1]\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"    # ANCHORED AT (0, 0.0) -- lora_B initialises to zeros, so ||dW|| is exactly 0\n    # before the first update. Without it f(0) clamps to the first sample's value\n    # and band 0 is understated by a DIFFERENT amount per arm (item 99).\n    t = [(0, 0.0)] + [q for q in t if q[0] > 0]\n    if len(t) < 2:\n        return None\n    x = [u for u, _ in t]\n    y = [v for _, v in t]\n    last = x[-1]\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1\np.write_text(s.replace(old, new)); print(\"anchored read_p15_posthoc\")\nPY\ntimeout 300 python diagnostics/read_ordering_axis.py 2>&1 | sed -n '/MATCHED on/,/wrong way/p' | tail -14","description":"Anchor p15 posthoc and recompute the table"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":771,"cache_read_input_tokens":336218,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":771},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"72689225-3a14-433b-a87c-a968fd02b419","request_id":"req_011CfAE51BwahRbZj2yPBqmj"}
