t per arm.** `bands()` interpolates `f(100) - f(0)`, but no arm has a drift sample at update 0, so `f(0)` clamped to the FIRST SAMPLE's value — 0.395 for p13 (u=33), 0.211 for p14 (u=19), 0.446 for p15 (u=38). Band 0 therefore came out **25%, 11% and 26% low**, and the size of the error was set by nothing but when each tracker happened to start. peft initialises `lora_B` to zeros, so ‖ΔW‖ is **exactly 0** before update 1 — the anchor is a fact about the adapter, not an assumption, and it was available all along. Corrected: d0 early/late **10.83 → 14.45**, d9 **1.14 → 1.29**, sep-9 ratio **9.49 → 11.24**, total gap **2.654 → 2.838**. *A per-arm bias is worse than a constant one: it survives every cross-arm comparison, which is the only kind this section makes. And it sat in the one cell the sep-9 pair rests on — the pair I had already flagged as the only LR-contaminated one, so I had looked straight at it twice.* Bands 1–9 are unaffected (both edges bracketed by real samples), so the four LR-matched pairs are unchanged | **an interpolation clamped to the first sample instead of to zero** |\\n\"]\nPath(\"RESEARCH-LOG.md\").write_text(\"\".join(L)); print(\"inserted ledger 99\")\nPY\npython3 -c \"\nimport pathlib,re\np=pathlib.Path('/workspace/trials/diagnostics/audit_log_numbers.py');s=p.read_text()\ns=s.replace('    \\\"schedule i never read\\\",\\n','    \\\"schedule i never read\\\",\\n    \\\"clamped to the first sample instead of to zero\\\",\\n')\np.write_text(s);print('marker enrolled')\"\ntimeout 600 python diagnostics/audit_log_numbers.py 2>&1 | grep -cE \"\\*\\*\\*\"; timeout 300 python diagnostics/check_log_structure.py --fix 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Correct the tool docstring, add ledger 99, re-audit"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":756,"cache_read_input_tokens":344225,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephe