{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U4WzmfyP2rJcSTAsiv5aoQ","type":"tool_result","content":"G  p13 vs T2     +0.00070  t=  0.16   p(t,2df)=0.8896\nG  p14 vs T2     -0.01864  t= -4.18   p(t,2df)=0.0526\nG  p13 vs p14    +0.01934  t=  4.34   p(t,2df)=0.0492\n\nAnd the DISTRIBUTION-FREE alternative, which needs no sigma at all:\n  the three ordering arms share identical composition, exposures and seed.\n  Under the null that order is irrelevant they are exchangeable, so the\n  probability they land in the SPECIFIC predicted order (easy > random >\n  hard) on R is 1/3! = 1/6 = 0.167 -- and the same order holds on G.\n  Both metrics agreeing on the same 1-in-6 ordering is the cleanest\n  assumption-light statement available, though R and G are not independent.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"504eb57b-7f05-4020-9eb5-f165fb69f3ba","timestamp":"2026-09-18T04:02:48.660Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"G  p13 vs T2     +0.00070  t=  0.16   p(t,2df)=0.8896\nG  p14 vs T2     -0.01864  t= -4.18   p(t,2df)=0.0526\nG  p13 vs p14    +0.01934  t=  4.34   p(t,2df)=0.0492\n\nAnd the DISTRIBUTION-FREE alternative, which needs no sigma at all:\n  the three ordering arms share identical composition, exposures and seed.\n  Under the null that order is irrelevant they are exchangeable, so the\n  probability they land in the SPECIFIC predicted order (easy > random >\n  hard) on R is 1/3! = 1/6 = 0.167 -- and the same order holds on G.\n  Both metrics agreeing on the same 1-in-6 ordering is the cleanest\n  assumption-light statement available, though R and G are not independent.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAEtA3KWGAqoESWRSErF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FQVWSaroKTFwLaXRbotTVx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/trials; python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"RESEARCH-LOG.md\"); t = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"`R` spans **0.01592 = 5.5σ**, `G` spans **0.01934 = 4.3σ**, against σ from the\nuniform lottery (2 df). Both metrics rank the three arms in the same order. **p13 is\nthe retained submission at agent-10, `S = 0.22340`** — the best arm in the log.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"`R` spans **0.01592**, `G` spans **0.01934**. Both metrics rank the three arms in\nthe same order. **p13 is the retained submission at agent-10, `S = 0.22340`** — the\nbest arm in the log.\n\n**Stated honestly, because σ here has 2 df and σ-multiples read like normal\np-values when they are not** (item 101). Referred to `t(2)`:\n\n| contrast | ΔR | p(t,2df) | ΔG | p(t,2df) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| p13 vs p14 | +0.01592 | **0.031** | +0.01934 | **0.049** |\n| p14 vs T2 | −0.01028 | 0.070 | −0.01864 | 0.053 |\n| p13 vs T2 | +0.00564 | 0.188 | +0.00070 | 0.890 |\n\nSo the **ordering axis** (p13 vs p14) is what carries this, on both metrics. **p13\nvs T2 on its own is not significant** and is not claimed to be; p13 is retained\nbecause `restore_best.sh` selects by score, which is a decision rule, not an\ninference. The distribution-free version needs no σ at all: three arms of identical\ncomposition, exposures and seed are exchangeable under \"order is irrelevant\", so\nlanding in the specific predicted order is p = 1/3! = **0.167** per metric — and\nboth metrics give the same order, though they are not independent.\"\"\"\nassert t.count(old) == 1\np.write_text(t.replace(old, new)); print(\"RESULT 2 corrected for df\")\n\nL = Path(\"RESEARCH-LOG.md\").read_text().splitlines(True)\ni = next(k for k, l in enumerate(L) if l.startswith(\"| 100 | **\\\"hq rows train\"))\nL[i:i] = [\"| 101 | **every σ-multiple in this log quoted against a σ with 2 df**, read as if normal. `sigma_R = 0.00203` and `sigma_G = 0.00315` each come from three uniform draws, so the reference distribution is `t(2)`, whose tails are far heavier. Corrected: p13 vs T2 on `R` is **1.96σ → p = 0.188, NOT significant** (I had been treating it as ≈0.05); p14 vs T2 is 3.58σ → p = 0.070; the p13-vs-p14 axis is 5.55σ → **p = 0.031**, and on `G` 4.34σ → **p = 0.049**. *The numbers were all correctly computed and correctly labelled \\\"sigma\\\"; what was wrong is that \\\"3.6 sigma\\\" carries an unearned p-value in the reader's head, and I am the reader. Ledger 67 already caught sigma_R being 24× wrong at 1 df — having learned that the ESTIMATE is fragile, I kept using it as though the DISTRIBUTION were not.* The ordering-axis conclusion survives; the p13-vs-T2 pairwise does not, and was never the claim. Distribution-free backstop added: three exchangeable arms landing in the predicted order is p = 1/6 per metric | **normal quantiles on a 2-df sigma** |\\n\"]\nPath(\"RESEARCH-LOG.md\").write_text(\"\".join(L))\nq = Path(\"/workspace/trials/diagnostics/audit_log_numbers.py\"); s = q.read_text()\ns = s.replace('    \"a premise used as a measurement\",\\n',\n              '    \"a premise used as a measurement\",\\n    \"normal quantiles on a 2-df sigma\",\\n')\nq.write_text(s)\nprint(\"inserted ledger 101 + marker\")\nPY\ntimeout 300 python diagnostics/check_log_structure.py --fix 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Correct significance claims and add ledger 101"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1425,"cache_read_input_tokens":367692,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1425},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"pa