{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019c8k6HqQCRfAeGS8DCFQUN","type":"tool_result","content":"71\tsleep 20\n72\techo \"== drift trackers ==\"\n73\t# TWO cadences, merged at read time by dedup on completed_updates.\n74\t#\n75\t# The coarse one is the original. The fine one was a manual afterthought during\n76\t# p14 (04:35), and it mattered: it took p14's coverage of the registered [700,899]\n77\t# window from 5 samples to 15, moving P(disjoint|no effect) from 0.0958 to 0.00010.\n78\t# p13 got two samples, and every p13-vs-anything geometric comparison is still\n79\t# bounded by that, permanently -- fixing an instrument helps only what comes after\n80\t# it. Starting both automatically is the difference between remembering and not:\n81\t# band-edge interpolation (read_p15.py limit 4) is only as good as the cadence,\n82\t# and no reading should depend on my having typed a second command.\n83\tnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n84\t  --interval 300 --samples 24 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &\n85\techo \"   coarse (300s) pid $!\"\n86\t# ABSOLUTE path, belt and braces. This exact line read `--out drift-fine.jsonl`\n87\t# and track_drift resolved it against the CALLER's cwd, so p15's fine samples went\n88\t# to /workspace/trials/drift-fine.jsonl -- a shared path no reader opens. It logged\n89\t# healthy samples throughout and nothing errored. track_drift now resolves bare\n90\t# names against --trial, which fixes it at the root; passing an absolute path here\n91\t# means this line is correct under either behaviour.\n92\tnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n93\t  --interval 120 --samples 60 --out \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}/drift-fine.jsonl\" \\\n94\t  >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift-fine.log\" 2>&1 &\n95\techo \"   fine   (120s) pid $!\"\n96\tsleep 10\n97\tfor F in drift.jsonl drift-fine.jsonl; do\n98\t  if [ -s \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}/$F\" ]; then echo \"   $F writing OK\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"0a716fca-43ad-47a1-a74f-12aa3b27eb8d","timestamp":"2026-09-18T04:18:20.678Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/trials/diagnostics/launch_next.sh","content":"sleep 20\necho \"== drift trackers ==\"\n# TWO cadences, merged at read time by dedup on completed_updates.\n#\n# The coarse one is the original. The fine one was a manual afterthought during\n# p14 (04:35), and it mattered: it took p14's coverage of the registered [700,899]\n# window from 5 samples to 15, moving P(disjoint|no effect) from 0.0958 to 0.00010.\n# p13 got two samples, and every p13-vs-anything geometric comparison is still\n# bounded by that, permanently -- fixing an instrument helps only what comes after\n# it. Starting both automatically is the difference between remembering and not:\n# band-edge interpolation (read_p15.py limit 4) is only as good as the cadence,\n# and no reading should depend on my having typed a second command.\nnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  --interval 300 --samples 24 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   coarse (300s) pid $!\"\n# ABSOLUTE path, belt and braces. This exact line read `--out drift-fine.jsonl`\n# and track_drift resolved it against the CALLER's cwd, so p15's fine samples went\n# to /workspace/trials/drift-fine.jsonl -- a shared path no reader opens. It logged\n# healthy samples throughout and nothing errored. track_drift now resolves bare\n# names against --trial, which fixes it at the root; passing an absolute path here\n# means this line is correct under either behaviour.\nnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  --interval 120 --samples 60 --out \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}/drift-fine.jsonl\" \\\n  >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift-fine.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   fine   (120s) pid $!\"\nsleep 10\nfor F in drift.