{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015gFZrLEC5dRx47RndciSoe","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /workspace/trials/diagnostics/launch_next.sh has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ddc471af-9d1d-4e5a-8360-478d04adf2fb","uuid":"b14fe67c-38b1-4839-9d7c-6736c9f60161","timestamp":"2026-09-18T04:18:29.061Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/workspace/trials/diagnostics/launch_next.sh","oldString":"nohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  --interval 300 --samples 24 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   coarse (300s) pid $!\"","newString":"# BUDGETS SIZED FOR 4 HOURS, NOT 2. Both caps used to give exactly 7200s of\n# coverage (300x24 and 120x60), and p15 ran longer than that. At update 800 the\n# coarse tracker had 8 samples left and the fine one 21, with ~40 min of training to\n# go -- so both would have expired within minutes of the finish, and the samples\n# they had left were being spent BEFORE bands 8-9, which hold the only replicate of\n# a band measurement in this log (band 8 had 1 sample, band 9 had 0).\n#\n# Nothing was failing. A --samples cap does not announce itself when it binds: the\n# process exits cleanly and simply stops producing rows, which looks exactly like a\n# finished run. Sampling is a checkpoint copy plus a small CPU matmul, so an\n# oversized budget costs almost nothing and an undersized one costs the measurement.\nnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  --interval 300 --samples 48 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   coarse (300s x48 = 4h) pid $!\"","originalFile":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Chain the scorer smoke test and the next training arm so the GPUs never idle\n# between them. The smoke test proves score_pool.py works on real hardware and\n# previews the margin distribution that decides the T5 tilt shape; its output is\n# read later, so there is no reason to wait for it before starting training.\n#\n# Usage: launch_next.sh <policy-name> <trial-name> [--skip-smoke]\nset -euo pipefail\n\nPOLICY=\"${1:?usage: launch_next.sh <policy-name> <trial-name> [--skip-smoke]}\"\nTRIAL=\"${2:?usage: launch_next.sh <policy-name> <trial-name> [--skip-smoke]}\"\nSKIP_SMOKE=\"${3:-}\"\n\nDIAG=/workspace/trials/diagnostics\nLOGS=/workspace/trials/logs\nmkdir -p \"$LOGS\"\n\n# Refuse to start if anything is already holding a GPU.\ngpu_procs() {\n  { pgrep -af '(python[0-9.]*|torchrun)[^|]*(train_trial\\.py|score_pool\\.py)' 2>/dev/null\n    for pid in $(nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps=pid --format=csv,noheader 2>/dev/null); do\n      ps -p \"$pid\" -o pid=,args= 2>/dev/null\n    done\n  } | sort -u || true\n}\nif [ -n \"$(gpu_procs)\" ]; then\n  echo \"ABORT: a Work GPU process is already running\"; gpu_procs; exit 1\nfi\n\n# The Judge may hold the GPUs from a different PID namespace, in which case\n# nvidia-smi reports memory but `ps -p` resolves nothing. Treat resident memory\n# as occupancy regardless of whether the owning process is visible here.\nBUSY=$(nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,memory.used --format=csv,noheader,nounits 2>/dev/null \\\n       | awk -F', *' '$2 > 2048 {printf \"GPU%s=%sMiB \", $1, $2}')\nif [ -n \"$BUSY\" ]; then\n  echo \"ABORT: GPU memory is in use by a process this container cannot see\"\n  echo \"       $BUSY\"\n  echo \"       This is most likely the Judge still evaluating a submission.\"\n  echo \"       Wait for it to finish, then re-run. Override only if you are sure:\"\n  echo \"         ALLOW_BUSY_GPU=1 $0 $*\"\n  [ \"${ALLOW_BUSY_GPU:-}\" = \"1\" ] || exit 1\n  echo \"       ALLOW_BUSY_GPU=1 set -- proceeding anyway\"\nfi\n\nif [ -d \"/workspace/trials/$TRIAL\" ] && [ ! -f \"/workspace/trials/$TRIAL/COMPLETE\" ]; then\n  echo \"NOTE: /workspace/trials/$TRIAL exists and is incomplete.\"\n  echo \"      run_fixed.py will RESUME it. If it died mid-update the journal is\"\n  echo \"      unrecoverable -- check first:\"\n  echo \"      python $DIAG/check_journal.py --trial /workspace/trials/$TRIAL\"\nfi\n\nif [ \"$SKIP_SMOKE\" != \"--skip-smoke\" ] && [ ! -f \"$DIAG/margins-smoke.rank0.npz\" ]; then\n  echo \"== scorer smoke test (~5 min, 5000 rows spanning hq and vl) ==\"\n  PYTHONPATH=/opt/reasonir-task/runtime torchrun --standalone --nproc-per-node=4 \\\n    \"$DIAG/score_pool.py\" \\\n    --out \"$DIAG/margins-smoke\" \\\n    --exclude /workspace/policy/scores-p02-label-anchor.jsonl \\\n    --sample 5000 --reference-size 4096 \\\n    > \"$LOGS/smoke-score.log\" 2>&1 \\\n    && echo \"   smoke test OK -> $LOGS/smoke-score.log\" \\\n    || echo \"   SMOKE TEST FAILED (training still proceeds) -- see $LOGS/smoke-score.log\"\nfi\n\necho \"== training $TRIAL with $POLICY (~2 h) ==\"\nnohup python /opt/reasonir-task/runtime/run_fixed.py \\\n  --policy \"/workspace/policy/${POLICY}.json\" \\\n  --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  > \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   launched pid $!  -> $LOGS/${TRIAL}.log\"\n\nsleep 20\necho \"== drift trackers ==\"\n# TWO cadences, merged at read time by dedup on completed_updates.\n#\n# The coarse one is the original. The fine one was a manual afterthought during\n# p14 (04:35), and it mattered: it took p14's coverage of the registered [700,899]\n# window from 5 samples to 15, moving P(disjoint|no effect) from 0.0958 to 0.00010.\n# p13 got two samples, and every p13-vs-anything geometric comparison is still\n# bounded by that, permanently -- fixing an instrument helps only what comes after\n# it. Starting both automatically is the difference between remembering and not:\n# band-edge interpolation (read_p15.py limit 4) is only as good as the cadence,\n# and no reading should depend on my having typed a second command.\nnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  --interval 300 --samples 24 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   coarse (300s) pid $!\"\n# ABSOLUTE path, belt and braces. This exact line read `--out drift-fine.jsonl`\n# and track_drift resolved it against the CALLER's cwd, so p15's fine samples went\n# to /workspace/trials/drift-fine.jsonl -- a shared path no reader opens. It logged\n# healthy samples throughout and nothing errored. track_drift now resolves bare\n# names against --trial, which fixes it at the root; passing an absolute path here\n# means this line is correct under either behaviour.\nnohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\\n  --interval 120 --samples 60 --out \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}/drift-fine.jsonl\" \\\n  >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift-fine.log\" 2>&1 &\necho \"   fine   (120s) pid $!\"\nsleep 10\nfor F in drift.jsonl drift-fine.jsonl; do\n  if [ -s \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}/$F\" ]; then echo \"   $F writing OK\"\n  else echo \"   WARNING: /workspace/trials/${TRIAL}/$F is empty or missing\"; fi\ndone\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":80,"oldLines":9,"newStart":80,"newLines":20,"lines":[" # it. Starting both automatically is the difference between remembering and not:"," # band-edge interpolation (read_p15.py limit 4) is only as good as the cadence,"," # and no reading should depend on my having typed a second command.","+# BUDGETS SIZED FOR 4 HOURS, NOT 2. Both caps used to give exactly 7200s of","+# coverage (300x24 and 120x60), and p15 ran longer than that. At update 800 the","+# coarse tracker had 8 samples left and the fine one 21, with ~40 min of training to","+# go -- so both would have expired within minutes of the finish, and the samples","+# they had left were being spent BEFORE bands 8-9, which hold the only replicate of","+# a band measurement in this log (band 8 had 1 sample, band 9 had 0).","+#","+# Nothing was failing. A --samples cap does not announce itself when it binds: the","+# process exits cleanly and simply stops producing rows, which looks exactly like a","+# finished run. Sampling is a checkpoint copy plus a small CPU matmul, so an","+# oversized budget costs almost nothing and an undersized one costs the measurement."," nohup python \"$DIAG/track_drift.py\" --trial \"/workspace/trials/${TRIAL}\" \\","-  --interval 300 --samples 24 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &","-echo \"   coarse (300s) pid $!\"","+  --interval 300 --samples 48 >> \"$LOGS/${TRIAL}-drift.log\" 2>&1 &","+echo \"   coarse (300s x48 = 4h) pid $!\""," # ABSOLUTE path, belt and braces. This exact line read `--out drift-fine.jsonl`"," # and track_drift resolved it against the CALLER's cwd, so p15's fine samples went"," # to /workspace/trials/drift-fine.jsonl -- a shared path no reader opens. It logged"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
